 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "manchester_cheetham_hill",
   "title": "Cheetham Hill",
   "text": "Jewish families from Germany, Russia, Poland and Lithuania settled in Cheetham Hill from the 1800s, many working in tailoring and the raincoat trade. The Spanish and Portuguese Synagogue on Cheetham Hill Road, built in 1874 in a Moorish style, is now the Manchester Jewish Museum.",
   "people": "jewish"
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "ALBERT SQUARE. The Town Hall clock keeps perfect time. The trams, less so."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "ANCOATS. The first industrial suburb on Earth. Please admire from the pavement. And bring a brolly."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "PICCADILLY GARDENS. Pigeons, pop-ups and, on Thursdays, a man who plays one note on a trumpet."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "BEV'S BIKES. Repairs, rentals and strong opinions about gears. Bring a puncture."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "THE ENCLOSURE HERITAGE EXPERIENCE. The countryside, curated for your convenience. Gift shop exit only."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "FREE TOTE BAG WITH EVERY SIGNATURE. Terms and conditions apply to your soul."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "PICCADILLY. Trains to everywhere, usually eventually."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "MAMUCIUM. The Romans built a fort here in AD 79. They were not told about the drizzle."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "MANCHESTER. Welcome to the city that invented the rain. Don't say we didn't warn you. Wait, we just did."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 56,
   "text": "SOUTH: the Goyt Valley and Buxton. Uphill the whole way. Eat first."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "WEST: Chat Moss, the A57 and eventually Liverpool."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 1,
   "text": "Road to Bolton closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Alan Turing, mathematician and computing pioneer, worked at the University of Manchester from 1948 to 1954.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "manchester_alan_turing",
   "title": "Alan Turing"
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "manchester_peterloo",
   "title": "Peterloo",
   "text": "On 16 August 1819 a huge, peaceful crowd met at St Peter's Field, close to here, to call for the vote. Cavalry charged into it with sabres; at least fifteen people died and hundreds were hurt. It was nicknamed Peterloo, after Waterloo, and it helped inspire the founding of the Manchester Guardian."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "manchester_john_dalton",
   "title": "John Dalton",
   "text": "John Dalton, a Quaker teacher from Cumberland, did his great work in Manchester. He argued that every element is made of its own kind of atom. He was colour blind, and studied that too, so it was long called Daltonism. When he died in 1844, Manchester gave him a huge public funeral."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "manchester_vimto",
   "title": "Vimto",
   "text": "Vimto was invented in Manchester in 1908 by John Noel Nichols, a wholesaler of herbs and spices, and sold first as a health tonic. It blends grape, raspberry and blackcurrant with a secret recipe that is still secret. It is hugely popular in the Middle East, especially during Ramadan."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "manchester_peppered_moth",
   "title": "The Peppered Moth",
   "text": "The peppered moth is normally pale and speckled, invisible on a lichen-covered tree. In 1848 a black one was recorded in Manchester, and as soot darkened the city's trees the dark moths took over, because birds could not spot them. It became a famous example of evolution in plain sight."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "EAST: Oldham, Saddleworth Moor and over the Pennines to Leeds. Open at last. Mind the hills."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 45,
   "plaque": "manchester_baby",
   "title": "The Baby",
   "text": "On 21 June 1948, at the University of Manchester, the Small-Scale Experimental Machine, known as the Baby, ran the first program ever held in a computer's own electronic memory. Built by Freddie Williams, Tom Kilburn and Geoff Tootill, it spent 52 minutes finding the highest factor of a number, and got it right. A working replica stands in the Science and Industry Museum."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "arts_lowry",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "L. S. Lowry",
   "text": "Laurence Stephen Lowry was born in Stretford in 1887 and spent his working life as a rent collector, painting in the evenings. His mill towns are white skies, red chimneys and crowds of thin dark figures, the matchstick men, hurrying to the football or the factory gate. He turned down five honours, including a knighthood, and died in 1976. The Lowry at Salford Quays, opened in 2000, holds the biggest collection of his work. He would have painted the queue."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "arts_halle",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Hallé",
   "text": "Charles Hallé, a pianist and conductor from Germany, gave the first concert of his orchestra at the Free Trade Hall on 30 January 1858, and the Hallé has played in Manchester every season since, one of the oldest professional orchestras in Britain. Since 1996 its home has been the Bridgewater Hall. Sir John Barbirolli conducted it for nearly thirty years from 1943. Please switch off your phone; the brass can hear it."
  }
 ],
